7 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555684 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555684 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555684 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555684 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555684 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "605de236-0714-5f9b-b01a-6122dd1e3831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555687 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555687 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555687 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555687 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "62f65add-d1f7-57d9-bd73-9ef3d190de02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555690 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555690 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555690 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555690 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555690 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555690 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555690 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555690 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555690 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "65924cba-b05a-57b8-addb-a6b67c843b8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555715 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555715 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555715 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555715 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555715 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9474a4f7-d9d7-5966-bcd5-2d786ecd797e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555754 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555754 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555754 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555754 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f3ba6166-7a5e-5458-bda8-6a3496ebc681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555789 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555789 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555789 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555789 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "382e043a-e5b7-50fb-a8b9-9a5ba4e6f38b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555789 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555820 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555820 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555820 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555820 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nIII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $V$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555820 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7135217a-2b68-531f-be98-4ee4582b3546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555820 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3})+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3},{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3},{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2}){b}_{3}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$, the given set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is false since the vector ${b}_{3}$ can't be written as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}.$ But then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & ={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555837 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555837 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555837 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555837 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{1,t,t+1\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nII. $2t+1$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{1,t,t+1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{1,t,t+1\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555837 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a623800-a9e7-562e-b4c1-2e4906af2084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555837 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot t+(-1)\\cdot (t+1)=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere all three coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2t+1=0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot t+1\\cdot (t+1).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555847 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555847 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555847 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555847 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555847 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab0273b9-503a-530e-8fea-6b6048661bee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555847 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555861 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555861 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555861 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555861 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nII. $3t-2{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555861 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da9b8a15-279d-5af5-bedd-335abf7ae54a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555861 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot t+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3t-2{t}^{3}=3\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the polynomial $1+t$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set\n$\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555871 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555871 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555871 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555871 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\nIII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555871 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4424f4e-4e2c-59a3-8d15-5c180b952f0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555871 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $\\{{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+{x}_{2}{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent. Hence, the given set is not a basis for $V.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{1}+{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{1}+{b}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
